Title: Perceus & FBIP
[INCLUDE=../sidebar-sub.mdk]

# Perceus & FBIP { .heading-no-number }

Perceus is an advanced compilation method for reference counting that enables **Functional But In-Place (FBIP)** programming.
This combination allows Koka to compile directly to C code without a garbage collector, while optimizing functional algorithms to use efficient in-place mutations.

## Perceus Reference Counting
Perceus is the compiler optimized reference counting technique that Koka
uses for automatic memory management. This (together
with evidence passing)
enables Koka to compile directly to plain C code without needing a
garbage collector or runtime system.

Perceus uses extensive static analysis to aggressively optimize the
reference counts. Here the strong semantic foundation of Koka helps a
lot: inductive data types cannot form cycles, and potential sharing
across threads can be reliably determined.

Normally we need to make a fundamental choice when managing memory: 

- We either use manual memory management (C, C++, Rust) and we get 
  the best performance but at a significant programming burden,
- Or, we use garbage collection (OCaml, C#, Java, Go, etc.) but
  but now we need a runtime system and pay a price in performance,
  memory usage, and unpredictable latencies.

With Perceus, we hope to cross this gap and our goal is to 
be within 2x of the performance of C/C++. Initial benchmarks are
encouraging and show Koka to be close to C performance on various
memory intensive benchmarks.

One concrete example is purely functional insertion into a red-black tree. Compiling the sample with
optimizations gives:

The sample lives in `samples/basic/rbtree.kk`, next to a `rbtree.cpp` comparison implementation.

```unchecked
$ koka -O2 -o kk-rbtree samples/basic/rbtree.kk
...
$ time ./kk-rbtree
420000
real    0m0.626s
```

Compared to a C++ implementation using `std::map`:

```unchecked
$ clang++ --std=c++17 -o cpp-rbtree -O3 .../samples/basic/rbtree.cpp
$ time ./cpp-rbtree
420000
real    0m0.667s
```

The point is not that Koka always beats C++, but that a purely functional implementation can end up with
very similar fast paths once Perceus has turned the unique case into mostly in-place updates.

[See benchmarks](https://github.com/koka-lang/koka#Benchmarks)
{.learn}

[Read the Perceus technical report](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/perceus-garbage-free-reference-counting-with-reuse/)
{.learn}

## Functional But In-Place (FBIP)

Perceus also performs _reuse analysis_ as part of reference
counting analysis. This pairs pattern matches with constructors of the
same size and reuses them _in-place_ if possible. Take for example,
the `map` function over lists:
```koka 
fun map-fbip( xs : list<a>, f : a -> e b ) : e list<b>
  match xs
    Cons(x,xx) -> Cons( f(x), map-fbip(xx,f) )
    Nil        -> Nil
```

Here the matched `Cons` can be reused by the new `Cons`
in the branch. This means if we map over a list that is not shared, 
like `list(1,100000).map(sqr).sum`,
then the list is updated _in-place_ without any extra allocation.
This is very effective for many functional style programs.

Moreover, the Koka compiler also implements _tail-recursion modulo cons_ (TRMC)
and instead of using a recursive call, the function is eventually optimized 
into an in-place updating loop for the fast path.

If you prefer an imperative mental model, TRMC is the step that turns a structurally recursive function into
the kind of tight loop you would normally write by hand in C, while preserving the functional source program.

Importantly, the reuse optimization is guaranteed
and a programmer can see when the optimization applies.
This leads to a new programming technique we call FBIP:
_functional but in-place_. Just like tail-recursion allows us
to express loops with regular function calls, reuse analysis 
allows us to express many imperative algorithms in a purely
functional style. 

Two classic examples are worth keeping in mind:

- **Tree rebalancing:** in the red-black tree sample, pattern matches and same-sized constructor allocations line up so unique trees can be rebalanced largely in place. In the fast path, matched `Node` constructors are effectively paired with rebuilt `Node` constructors of the same size.
- **Morris-style traversal:** even tree traversals that would normally need an explicit stack can often be reformulated so reuse analysis turns the fast path into a tight loop with no extra heap allocation or extra traversal stack.

### Tree Rebalancing in More Detail

The red-black tree sample is useful because it is both a standard purely functional algorithm and a good stress test
for reuse analysis. A red-black tree maintains two invariants:

- every root-to-leaf path has the same number of black nodes
- a red node never has a red child

Together these invariants keep the tree balanced. Okasaki-style functional insertion maintains them by rebuilding only
the local path around the inserted node and rebalancing when needed. In source form this looks like ordinary persistent
functional code; after inlining the balancing helpers, the important property is that matched `Node` constructors line up
with rebuilt `Node` constructors of the same size.

That is exactly the shape Perceus can exploit. In the unique case, the fast path reuses those `Node`s in place, so the
generated code looks much closer to an imperative rebalancing routine than the source would suggest. In the persistent
case, the same program still behaves correctly by copying only the shared spine and reusing any unshared parts.

### Morris Traversal, Functionally

An in-order tree walk is another good FBIP example because the obvious functional version is not tail-recursive and would
normally use stack space proportional to tree depth. Historically, Knuth posed the problem of doing in-order traversal with
no extra stack or heap, and Morris gave an elegant imperative solution that temporarily rewires the tree through
right-threading. That imperative algorithm is subtle because its correctness depends on invariants over intermediate graph
shapes, not just ordinary tree structure.

The FBIP approach keeps the same operational goal but makes the intermediate structure explicit. Instead of mutating
pointers to remember where to return, we model the pending work as a visitor value. Conceptually, this is a zipper-like
representation of the rest of the traversal state.

The important insight for reuse analysis is that the traversal progresses through same-sized states:

- `Bin` nodes are visited into `BinR`
- `BinR` states become `BinL`
- `BinL` states reconstruct `Bin`

Because these states are all the same size, the unique fast path can overlay them on the same memory. Combined with tail
calls, this lets the compiler generate a tight loop with no extra heap allocation and no extra traversal stack, while the
source remains purely functional and still adapts gracefully to shared input trees.

[Learn more about how to ensure your code is fbip](../guides/fbip.html){.learn}

[Read the paper on fully in-place functional programming](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/fbip-functional-but-in-place-programming/)
{.learn}

[Read the paper on generalized tail-recursion modulo cons](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/generalized-tail-recursion-modulo-cons/)
{.learn}

